## Answer

**No.** It is not possible for a coordinate system $f: M \to \mathbb{R}^n$ to satisfy $df_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge df_n(p) = 0$ at any point $p$ in its domain.

### Proof

Recall the definition. A **coordinate system** (or chart) on a smooth $n$-manifold $M$ is a pair $(U, f)$ where $U \subseteq M$ is open and $f = (f_1, \dots, f_n): U \to \mathbb{R}^n$ is a **diffeomorphism** onto an open subset $f(U) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$.

The defining property of a diffeomorphism is that its differential is a linear isomorphism at every point. Concretely, $f: U \to f(U)$ is a diffeomorphism if and only if $f$ is smooth, bijective, has a smooth inverse, and the differential

$$df_p : T_p M \longrightarrow T_{f(p)}\mathbb{R}^n \cong \mathbb{R}^n$$

is an isomorphism for every $p \in U$.

Now, the differential $df_p$ is represented (in any bases) by the Jacobian matrix whose rows are $df_1(p), \dots, df_n(p)$. The map $df_p$ is an isomorphism if and only if this Jacobian is nonsingular, i.e., the covectors $df_1(p), \dots, df_n(p)$ are **linearly independent** in $T_p^* M$.

The wedge product $df_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge df_n(p)$ is nonzero if and only if $df_1(p), \dots, df_n(p)$ are linearly independent. This is the standard characterization of the wedge product: for covectors $\alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_k$,

$$\alpha_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge \alpha_k \neq 0 \iff \alpha_1, \dots, \alpha_k \text{ are linearly independent.}$$

Therefore:

$$df_p \text{ is an isomorphism} \iff df_1(p), \dots, df_n(p) \text{ linearly independent} \iff df_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge df_n(p) \neq 0.$$

Since $f$ being a coordinate system **requires** $df_p$ to be an isomorphism at **every** $p \in U$, it follows that $df_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge df_n(p) \neq 0$ for every $p \in U$.

Hence the condition $df_1 \wedge \cdots \wedge df_n(p) = 0$ is **incompatible** with $f$ being a coordinate system. If the wedge vanishes at $p$, then $df_p$ is singular, $f$ fails to be a local diffeomorphism at $p$, and $(U, f)$ is not a valid coordinate system on $M$. $\blacksquare$

